.el .ds Aq '
.TH engraver 1  "engraver 0.2.0"
.SH NAME
engraver \- A safe, fast tool for creating bootable USB drives, SD cards, and NVMe drives
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBengraver\fR [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-silent\fR] [\fB\-\-config\-file\fR \fI<PATH>\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] <\fIcommand\fR>
.SH DESCRIPTION
Engraver is a fast, safe command-line tool for writing disk images to USB drives,
SD cards, and NVMe drives. It supports local files, HTTP/HTTPS URLs, and cloud
storage sources (S3, GCS, Azure Blob) when built with the \fBcloud\fR feature,
which the pre-built binaries leave out. Compressed images (.gz, .xz, .zst, .bz2)
are automatically decompressed during write.
.PP
Engraver includes multiple safety mechanisms: system drive detection prevents
accidental overwrites, confirmation prompts require explicit consent, and
parallel verification can hash data during the write pass for fast integrity checks.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Enable verbose output with additional diagnostic information.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Hide progress and status output. What a command is run to show still prints:
the hash from \fBchecksum\fR, the drives from \fBlist\fR, the settings or path from
\fBconfig\fR, and the results from \fBbenchmark\fR. Errors still print, and so do
confirmation prompts, with the image and drive they ask about. The \fBquiet\fR
setting under \fB[behavior]\fR in the configuration file turns it on too.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-quiet\fR
Show progress and status output, even if the configuration file sets
\fBquiet\fR. If you give both \fB\-\-quiet\fR and \fB\-\-no\-quiet\fR, the
last one wins, on either side of the subcommand. \fB\-\-silent\fR still hides
the output.
.TP
\fB\-\-silent\fR
Like \fB\-\-quiet\fR, and also hide the output of \fBlist\fR, \fBconfig\fR and
\fBbenchmark\fR, skip every confirmation prompt (implying \fB\-\-yes\fR, so it can't be used with
\fB\-\-confirm\fR), and hide the messages that Ctrl+C prints. What a script asks for still prints: the hash
from \fBchecksum\fR, the path from \fBconfig \-\-path\fR, and the JSON from every
\fB\-\-json\fR option.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\-file\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Use a custom configuration file instead of the default location.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help information.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version information.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
\fBengraver\-list\fR(1)
List available removable drives with metadata (size, model, USB speed).
.TP
\fBengraver\-write\fR(1)
Write an image to a drive with optional verification and resume support.
.TP
\fBengraver\-verify\fR(1)
Verify a drive's contents against a source image.
.TP
\fBengraver\-checksum\fR(1)
Calculate the checksum of an image file.
.TP
\fBengraver\-benchmark\fR(1)
Benchmark write speed of a drive (destructive).
.TP
\fBengraver\-erase\fR(1)
Erase a drive by zero-filling the entire device.
.TP
\fBengraver\-config\fR(1)
View and manage persistent configuration settings.
.TP
\fBengraver completions\fR \fI<SHELL>\fR
Generate shell completions for bash, zsh, fish, powershell, or elvish.
.TP
\fBengraver mangen\fR [\fB\-o\fR \fI<DIR>\fR]
Generate man pages in roff format.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\fB0\fR
Success. Answering no when asked to write, erase or benchmark prints
\fBAborted.\fR and also exits 0.
.TP
\fB1\fR
Error, such as permission denied, device not found, or a failed verification.
An option value that Engraver checks itself, such as
\fB\-\-algorithm nope\fR or \fB\-\-block\-size nope\fR, also exits 1.
.TP
\fB2\fR
Usage error, such as an unknown option or a missing argument.
.TP
\fB130\fR
Cancelled with Ctrl+C (SIGINT), including at a confirmation prompt.
A cancelled write or erase can leave the target partly written.
.SH EXAMPLES
List available drives:
.PP
.nf
    engraver list
.fi
.PP
Write an ISO to a USB drive with verification:
.PP
.nf
    engraver write ubuntu.iso /dev/sdb \-\-verify
.fi
.PP
Write from a URL with checkpointing for resume support:
.PP
.nf
    engraver write https://example.com/image.iso /dev/sdb \-\-checkpoint
.fi
.PP
Benchmark a drive to find the optimal block size:
.PP
.nf
    engraver benchmark /dev/sdb \-\-test\-block\-sizes "4K,64K,1M,4M"
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB~/.config/engraver/engraver_config.toml\fR
Configuration file on Linux, or
.br
\fB\%$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/engraver/engraver_config.toml\fR if \fB\%XDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is set.
See \fBengraver\-config\fR(1).
.TP
\fB~/Library/Application Support/engraver/engraver_config.toml\fR
Configuration file on macOS.
.TP
\fB~/.local/state/engraver/checkpoints/\fR
Checkpoint files for resume support (Linux/macOS).
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBengraver\-write\fR(1), \fBengraver\-list\fR(1), \fBengraver\-verify\fR(1), \fBengraver\-checksum\fR(1), \fBengraver\-benchmark\fR(1), \fBengraver\-erase\fR(1), \fBengraver\-config\fR(1)
.PP
Shell completions can be generated with \fBengraver completions\fR \fI<SHELL>\fR.
Man pages can be generated with \fBengraver mangen\fR.
